levin

A search harness for one hard, formally stated problem. You give it a Lean 4
statement and a token budget. It keeps a tree of reductions Lean has checked,
holds a belief about every node, and spends each task where it most raises the
chance the statement gets settled. It stops with a certificate the Lean kernel
has replayed, or with the budget spent.

Every task is an agent running in an inlet cell
(https://github.com/teddytennant/inlet), with no network and a metered model
proxy. That is how one Claude subscription runs it: inlet's proxy sits in front
of the subscription and charges each task against its own budget.


How it searches

A claim is a closed Lean proposition. A sketch is a way to prove a claim from
lemmas, which are claims too, keyed by their text, so a lemma two sketches
need is one node and gets proved once. A sketch may define new objects
(`def d_...`) that its lemmas talk about. Its glue, the Lean proof
`theorem (h1 : L1) ... (hk : Lk) : claim := body`, may come with it or later:
open problems get solved informally first, so a sketch whose lemmas Lean
accepts as propositions enters the tree informal, its lemmas get worked on,
and a formalize task writes the glue. Until then it counts at a discount
that drops each time the glue fails, so a plan that does not hold together
loses its compute. The root is P and
not-P together, since an open problem may be false, or P with ANSWER filled in
by each guessed value when the question asks for one.

Six kinds of task:

    prove      write a proof of the claim
    falsify    look for a counterexample, with python, and prove the negation
    decompose  write an informal proof, then a sketch
    formalize  write the glue of an informal sketch
    search     improve a construction against the problem's own scorer
    guess      propose a value for ANSWER

search is for problems where the answer is an object: a problem file with a
"score" (a command that rates a candidate file and prints a number, and a
target) gets search tasks on the side a construction would settle. The best
four candidates and their scores are kept on the claim and handed to the next
search task to improve, in the manner of AlphaEvolve; one that reaches the
target goes into the claim's notes for the proof that uses it. A problem file
with a "goal" instead of a "statement" is search only: no Lean, just the
scorer, which is how levin runs on an optimization contest. Search runs on
a root side only, not on lemmas.

Every new lemma gets a falsify task before any prove or decompose is spent on
it; when that runs is up to its score, like everything else. Decompositions written by models are full of plausible lemmas
that are false, and a false lemma takes down everything built on it. A prove
task that finds its claim false can hand in a proof of the negation instead,
and when a lemma is refuted its counterexample goes into the notes of every
claim that needed it, so the next decompose knows why the last plan died.

For each claim levin holds t, the chance it is true, and p, the chance one
prove task lands if it is. p is a Beta that starts at `prove_rate` (the root
uses `root_rate`, which is how you tell it the problem is hard) and counts
failures. A failed proof is one observation with likelihood 1 - t*p, so t and
p both drop; surviving a counterexample search raises t; a refuted lemma goes
to 0. Lemma priors come from the credence the decomposer states for each one,
capped at 0.9. Once every lemma of a sketch has survived its search, the claim
is at least as likely true as all of them together, so its t is raised to
their product and the other side of the root loses the same amount.

S(c), the chance c gets proved, combines direct attempts (t times the chance
`horizon` more of them land) with each sketch (the product of its lemmas) as
if they were independent. S(root) adds the resolutions up. One backward pass
gives dS(root)/dS(c) for every claim, and each possible task is scored as that
gradient times what the task would add, times its chance of working, divided
by its tokens. The best tasks fill the free slots, one per claim. That is
steepest ascent on the chance of a resolution, per token.

This is Levin search with beliefs in place of program lengths. Levin gives
each candidate time in proportion to its prior and keeps going; levin gives
each hypothesis (each claim, each way of splitting it) compute in proportion
to its posterior value per token, and every verdict from Lean updates the
posterior. Solomonoff's prior is not computable. The stand-in here is the
problem file's priors and the model's own stated credences.

S treats sketches and lemmas as independent. They are not: lemmas of one
sketch tend to fail together when the approach is wrong. It is a scoring
rule for where to look next, not a calibrated probability.

A swarm of independent agents repeats the same failed ideas and throws away
partial progress. Here partial progress is a checked reduction or a proved
lemma that every later task can cite by name, failed attempts leave notes the
next attempt on that claim reads, and false branches are cut early.


What it trusts

A worker controls a proof body, a sketch, glue, an answer or a candidate,
never the statement.
levin writes the file around it. Before Lean runs, text is refused if it has
a string or name literal or a `#`, declares anything, adds syntax or axioms,
runs meta code, or sets an option other than heartbeats; lemma statements may
not contain `by`. `autoImplicit` is off. Each lemma must elaborate as a Prop,
and a sketch is refused if any one lemma gives the claim by `simpa` alone, so
splitting cannot regress for free; that includes a lemma that is the claim
unfolded through a new definition. A definition must be named `d_...`, a
name nothing has used and the root statement does not mention, so it can add
an object but never change what the question says. A guessed answer must be an integer, or a
fraction when the problem file sets "answer_type" to ℚ or ℝ: anything richer
can restate the question (`sInf {n | P n}`) or splice into it. So questions
whose answer is √2 or π/4 are out of reach of `guess`; state those as yes/no.

The file is then compiled as a module and the module is replayed through
the kernel by leanchecker. A second Lean process imports it, re-elaborates
levin's own statement in a clean scope, checks the proved constant against
it, and reads its axioms; nothing past propext, Classical.choice and
Quot.sound passes. Whatever the worker's text did to its own scope stays in
its module. That second file gets the preamble's one-line scope commands
(open, namespace, section, variable, notation, set_option) but not its
imports or declarations, so keep a preamble to imports and such lines. The keyword filter is the cheap first layer and is known to be
bypassable: in review, a `run_tac` hidden behind a `"--"` string added an
unchecked `1 = 2` with `debug.skipKernelTC`. Strings are refused now, and the
replay catches that declaration by itself either way (tests/test_check.py).

The worker's own cell runs this gate so its retries get Lean's errors, but
the worker can edit files there. So every artifact is judged again in a fresh
cell from levin's own copy of the statement, and nothing a model wrote is
compiled on the host. A judge that dies of something other than a verdict is
asked again rather than counted. When the root closes, the proofs it depends
on are assembled into cert.lean and judged the same way. "fresh": true in the
problem file replays every import too, which for Mathlib takes hours.

Lean's documentation names Comparator as the tool meant for hostile proofs.
Before you believe a resolution of anything that matters, check cert.lean
with Comparator against the statement, and have a person read the formal
statement against the informal problem, and any guessed answer against the
question. A fast resolution of an open problem is more likely a
misformalized statement than a breakthrough.


Install

Python 3.8 or newer, nothing else from pip. inlet, and a Lean 4 toolchain whose
`lean` and `leanchecker` are visible inside a cell (/nix/store or a path listed
in the cell's `ro`). For a real problem, a lake project with Mathlib built, so
that `lake env lean` works there; with elan, list ~/.elan in `ro` too.

Against Mathlib v4.29.0 through `lake env`, one node (build, kernel replay of
the module, statement and axioms in a second process) took 27 to 64 seconds
and peaked at 6.1GB resident, which is why tasks default to `memory_mb` 8192.
Mind that when choosing `slots`.

    git clone https://github.com/teddytennant/levin
    cd levin
    python3 -m unittest discover tests       # LEVIN_LEAN=/path/to/lean runs the Lean tests too


Run

Two workers in the inlet policy: the agent that does the math, and a judge
that does nothing itself, so its verifier (the gate) is the whole task.

    cell    = { ro = { "/path/to/levin", "/path/to/mathlib-project" } }
    workers = {
      prover = { cmd = { ... any agent with a shell ... }, tags = { "math" }, net = "none" },
      levin_check = { cmd = { "true" }, tags = { "math" }, net = "none" },
    }

inlet's docs/harnesses.md has working `cmd` lines for Claude Code, Pi, Wizard
and others, and examples/claude-upstream.py puts a Claude login behind the
proxy. Then a problem file:

    {
      "statement": "∀ n : ℕ, ...",
      "preamble": "import Mathlib",
      "truth": 0.5,
      "root_rate": 0.001,
      "lean": ["lake", "env", "lean"],
      "leanchecker": ["lake", "env", "leanchecker"],
      "lean_dir": "/path/to/mathlib-project",
      "hints": "Mathlib source is at /path/to/mathlib-project/.lake/packages/mathlib.",
      "budget": 20000000,
      "inlet": {"home": "~/.inlet", "slots": 2, "memory_mb": 8192}
    }

Add "score": {"cmd": ["python3", "score.py"], "target": 1, "proves":
"negation"} for a problem a construction can settle. Per-task token budgets
default to agent size (400k for prove, decompose, formalize and search) and
go under "tokens".

and

    python3 -m levin init problem.json run/
    python3 -m levin run run/
    python3 -m levin show run/

`python3 -m levin curve run/` prints tokens spent against the best score so
far, which is the number to compare between arms. `run` can be killed and
started again at any time. Everything is in
run/events.jsonl and the tree is rebuilt from it; tasks still on inlet are
collected when they finish. `show` prints the chance of a resolution, the next
steps with their scores, and every claim.

The other knobs, with their defaults, are at the top of levin/tree.py: lemma
prior, prove rate, how much the priors weigh against evidence, horizon, depth,
sketches per claim (only sketches with no refuted lemma count), lemmas per
sketch. `catch` (the chance a falsify task finds a counterexample to a false
claim) and `guess_value` are policy, not measurements; nothing learns them.
`retries` (default 1) and `workers` (a worker per task kind, say a stronger
model for decompose) go under "inlet". Only a verifier's no, a used-up budget, a timeout or a stall
counts as evidence. A task that died some other way or used no tokens, which
is what a rate-limited subscription looks like, changes no belief, and three
in a row pause the run for 15 minutes.

Budgets are in whatever the proxy meters. Through claude-upstream.py every
call carries the CLI's own system prompt, about 28k tokens of it cached, so
size budgets for that.


Measuring it

Whether weighing hypotheses beats more tries is a question for an
experiment, not this README. The arms, all on one budget, on problems where a
single attempt almost never lands:

    best-of-N        "max_sketches": 0, "falsify": false, "notes": false
    one long agent   "max_sketches": 0, "slots": 1, one huge "prove" budget
    levin            defaults
    no beliefs       "planner": "round_robin" (same task kinds, no gradient)
    no falsify       "falsify": false (no counterexample searches at all)

Report solve rate against tokens. The round-robin arm is the one that says
whether the beliefs earn anything over lemma reuse and notes alone.

bench/run.py runs the best-of-N, levin and round-robin arms on a file of
statements, one budget for all three, and prints what each settled and what
it spent. Two runs of it so far, core Lean 4.29 with no Mathlib, Claude Haiku
through claude-upstream.py as the worker (inlet's examples/agent.py, 30
turns), 1.2M tokens per arm, 200k per prove task:

    statement                                    best-of-N    levin  round-robin
    ∀ a b, (a*a + b*b) % 4 ≠ 3                    yes 27k   yes 15k     yes 24k
    ∀ n, 6 ∣ n*(n+1)*(n+2)                        yes 22k   yes 35k     yes 10k
    ∀ n, 3 ∣ n^3 + 2*n                            yes 15k   yes 20k     yes 11k
    ∀ a b, (a*a + b*b) % 3 = 0 → a % 3 = 0        yes 19k   yes 17k     yes 19k
    ∀ n, 30 ∣ n^5 - n                             yes 25k   yes 24k     yes 24k
    ∀ a b c, a*a + b*b = c*c → 2 ∣ a*b            yes 47k   yes 32k     yes 20k

    ∀ x y, x*x = 2*(y*y) → x = 0                  yes 46k   yes 74k     yes 26k
    ∀ n, 4 ≤ n → n*n ≤ 2^n                        yes 24k   yes 29k     yes 35k
    Σ i<n, i^3 = (Σ i<n, i)^2                     yes 33k   yes 59k     yes 27k
    ∀ n > 1, n^4 + 4 has a proper factorization   yes 36k   yes 99k     yes 86k
    6 Σ i≤n, i^2 = n(n+1)(2n+1)                   yes 150k  yes 142k    yes 65k
    ∀ a b, 0 < b → a*a ≠ 3*(b*b)                  yes 30k   yes 64k     yes 22k

Every arm settled every statement with its first task, so these runs say
nothing about the planner: one attempt was enough, and the arms differ only
by the luck of that one attempt. What they do show is that levin runs no
extra tasks when a direct proof lands. The comparison that matters needs
problems a single attempt almost never solves.


Where to point it

Start where answers are known, miniF2F or PutnamBench, and check that the
priors are sane: the default `root_rate` of 0.3 is for an easy theorem. Then
open problems that are already stated in Lean, such as
github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures, pinned to one commit. Put what
the literature already says about the problem in "hints": some "open" problems
turned out to be solved in a paper nobody had connected to them.


Runs on this box

Lean 4.29.0 from nixpkgs, Claude Sonnet through claude-upstream.py, inlet's
examples/agent.py as the worker, two slots, no Mathlib.

`∀ n : Nat, n * n % 4 = 0 ∨ n * n % 4 = 1`, as an open question (truth 0.5).
Both sides were tried at once. The first try at the true side ran out of
budget; the first try at the false side failed and moved belief to the true
side; the second try at the true side landed. The judge accepted it, the
leftover task on the false side was killed, and the certificate replayed.
Four tasks, 418k metered tokens.

`∀ a b : Nat, (a * a + b * b) % 4 ≠ 3` with `root_rate` 0.001, so a direct
proof looked hopeless and the planner had to split it. Twice it did, by
different routes. The first came back as

    lemmas: ∀ n : Nat, n * n % 4 = 0 ∨ n * n % 4 = 1    (credence 0.99)
    body:   by intro a b; have ha := h1 a; have hb := h1 b; omega

Its lemma survived a counterexample search, was proved, and closed the
root. Eight tasks, 862k tokens. A rerun after the gate was rebuilt closed it
through its second sketch instead, whose lemma was the sharper
`∀ n : Nat, (n * n) % 4 = n % 2`: six tasks, 437k.

A fourth run, of the first problem on the finished code, had the task on the
false side hand in a proof of ¬¬P instead: 52k tokens. It also found a bug:
a second proof of an already settled root put a duplicate theorem in the
certificate. With that fixed, `levin run` on the same directory rebuilt the tree
from the log and certified it without new work.

These are small, and the model could have proved both directly. What they
show is the machinery: both sides of a question, beliefs moving on evidence,
verified splits, falsify before prove, the judge in a fresh cell, killed
leftovers, and a certificate the kernel replayed.
